WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:10.180
Every mark on these sheets was made by somebody reading. So what did they keep? Let's find out, with a book short enough to read in thirty seconds.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:22.980
Treat capitals and lowercase as the same word, even on printed sheets. "The" and "the" go in the same row. Full stops and exclamation marks count as words too.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:38.800
Now read it two words at a time. Find the row for the first word. The second word goes in a box on that row, with a mark beside it. Then slide along by one, and do it again.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:51.180
A new word after the same word takes the next empty box. And when a pair comes round again, it gets another mark. By the end, "the fence" has six marks, and "the magpie" five.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:59.180
For our model, training means counting pairs. Here's the rest of the book, counted by a computer.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:07.680
We can put the book away now. The sheets have counts for each pair of words, and that's what we'll use to write.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:15.520
And a row of marks is just a row of numbers. The row for "the" says five, six, four, four.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:25.260
Large language models also have numbers called weights. Those weights are adjusted during training to improve predictions; they aren't counts of word pairs.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:34.860
We can count a bigger book the same way. This is Frankenstein. Eighty-five thousand words, and seven thousand rows.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:44.420
Large language models train on much more text, learning patterns that help predict what comes next. Our counts let us try prediction by hand.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:02:01.840
Three things catch people out. A full stop is a word, and its row says how sentences start. A new word goes in the next empty box, never skip one. And say a new word out loud when you start its row, so nobody starts it twice.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.120
Reader, grab the text. Two words at a time.
